//! run
//! abort
//! stderr: runtime error: shift count -1 out of range for i32
//! stdout:
//| before
// The shifted operand is an untyped constant that no context reaches, so it
// takes its default type (D4.5). A shift whose count did not fold keeps the
// left operand's own type and leaves the count to the run time (D6.2), and the
// message names that type (toolchain.md 6 item 15). That is where the default
// type of `1` is visible from outside the compiler: it reads i32, not i64.
//
// It is 015_negative_shift_count.ft with one line taken out. That test gives
// the operand a type of its own, `i32 v = 1;`, so it holds the message and the
// count rule; this one writes the constant into the shift, so the type in the
// message is the one D4.5 chose and nothing else decides it. Neither test
// stands for the other: change 015 and the count rule loses its witness,
// change this one and the default type does.
//
// The constant here fits i32, so this test pins the fitting half of D4.5. The
// wide half is
// 033_shift_of_a_wide_default_typed_constant.ft, which is this file with
// `4294967296` in place of `1` and i64 in place of i32 in the message. Until
// T-117 that program was refused with "constant 4294967296 does not fit i32"
// in both compilers.
fn i32 minus_one() {
    return -1;
}

fn i32 main() {
    i32 n = minus_one();
    println("before");
    println(1 << n);
    return 0;
}
